Task 1: Label Application Namespaces for Service Mesh
Subtask 1.1: Create Application Namespaces
First, let's create the namespaces that will host our applications.

Create a new namespace called bookinfo:
kubectl create namespace bookinfo
Create another namespace called sample-app:
kubectl create namespace sample-app
Verify the namespaces were created successfully:
kubectl get namespaces
You should see both bookinfo and sample-app in the list of namespaces.

Subtask 1.2: Understanding Namespace Labels
Before we label our namespaces, let's understand what we're doing:

Namespace labels are key-value pairs that provide metadata about namespaces
Istio uses specific labels to identify which namespaces should have automatic sidecar injection
The label istio-injection=enabled tells Istio to automatically inject Envoy sidecars into pods
Subtask 1.3: Label Namespaces for Istio Injection
Label the bookinfo namespace for automatic sidecar injection:
kubectl label namespace bookinfo istio-injection=enabled
Label the sample-app namespace for automatic sidecar injection:
kubectl label namespace sample-app istio-injection=enabled
Verify the labels were applied correctly:
kubectl get namespace bookinfo --show-labels
kubectl get namespace sample-app --show-labels
You should see istio-injection=enabled in the labels for both namespaces.

Task 2: Enable Automatic Sidecar Injection for Namespaces
Subtask 2.1: Understand Sidecar Injection Methods
Istio provides two methods for sidecar injection:

Automatic Injection: Uses Kubernetes admission controllers to automatically inject sidecars
Manual Injection: Requires manual modification of deployment YAML files
We'll focus on automatic injection, which is more convenient for production environments.

Subtask 2.2: Verify Istio Sidecar Injector is Running
Check if the Istio sidecar injector is running:
kubectl get pods -n istio-system | grep sidecar-injector
Verify the sidecar injector webhook configuration:
kubectl get mutatingwebhookconfiguration istio-sidecar-injector -o yaml
This webhook automatically modifies pod specifications to include the Envoy sidecar container.

Subtask 2.3: Deploy Sample Applications
Now let's deploy applications to test automatic sidecar injection.

Create a simple nginx deployment in the bookinfo namespace:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: nginx-deployment
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
EOF
Create a simple httpd deployment in the sample-app namespace:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: httpd-deployment
  namespace: sample-app
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: httpd
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: httpd
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: httpd
        image: httpd:2.4
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
EOF
Wait for deployments to be ready:
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-deployment -n bookinfo
kubectl rollout status deployment/httpd-deployment -n sample-app
Task 3: Verify Envoy Proxy Sidecar is Injected in Pods
Subtask 3.1: Check Pod Container Count
When sidecar injection is successful, each pod should have two containers instead of one:

The original application container
The Envoy proxy sidecar container
Check pods in the bookinfo namespace:
kubectl get pods -n bookinfo
Look at the READY column. You should see 2/2 instead of 1/1, indicating two containers per pod.

Check pods in the sample-app namespace:
kubectl get pods -n sample-app
Again, you should see 2/2 in the READY column.

Subtask 3.2: Inspect Pod Details
Get detailed information about a pod in the bookinfo namespace:
# Get the pod name first
NGINX_POD=$(kubectl get pods -n bookinfo -l app=nginx -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')

# Describe the pod to see container details
kubectl describe pod $NGINX_POD -n bookinfo
Look for the Envoy sidecar container:
In the output, you should see two containers:

nginx (your application container)
istio-proxy (the Envoy sidecar container)
Check container names directly:
kubectl get pod $NGINX_POD -n bookinfo -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'
This should output: nginx istio-proxy

Subtask 3.3: Verify Sidecar Injection Annotations
Check for Istio injection annotations:
kubectl get pod $NGINX_POD -n bookinfo -o yaml | grep -A 5 -B 5 "sidecar.istio.io"
You should see annotations like:

sidecar.istio.io/status
sidecar.istio.io/inject
Verify the sidecar status annotation:
kubectl get pod $NGINX_POD -n bookinfo -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.sidecar\.istio\.io/status}'
This should return a JSON object showing the injected containers and volumes.

Subtask 3.4: Test Sidecar Functionality
Check if the Envoy admin interface is accessible:
kubectl exec $NGINX_POD -n bookinfo -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/help
This should return help information for the Envoy admin interface.

Check Envoy configuration:
kubectl exec $NGINX_POD -n bookinfo -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/config_dump | head -20
This shows the Envoy proxy configuration, confirming the sidecar is working.

Subtask 3.5: Compare with Non-Injected Namespace
To better understand the difference, let's create a pod in a namespace without sidecar injection.

Create a namespace without the injection label:
kubectl create namespace no-injection
Deploy a simple pod in this namespace:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: test-deployment
  namespace: no-injection
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: test
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: test
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
EOF
Compare the pod container count:
kubectl get pods -n no-injection
You should see 1/1 in the READY column, indicating only one container (no sidecar).

Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Sidecar Not Injected
Symptoms: Pods show 1/1 instead of 2/2 containers

Solutions:

Verify namespace label:
kubectl get namespace bookinfo --show-labels
Check if the label is correct:
kubectl label namespace bookinfo istio-injection=enabled --overwrite
Restart the deployment:
kubectl rollout restart deployment/nginx-deployment -n bookinfo
Issue 2: Pods Stuck in Pending State
Symptoms: Pods remain in Pending status

Solutions:

Check pod events:
kubectl describe pod $NGINX_POD -n bookinfo
Verify cluster resources:
kubectl top nodes
Issue 3: Sidecar Injector Not Working
Symptoms: No sidecars injected despite correct labels

Solutions:

Check sidecar injector pod status:
kubectl get pods -n istio-system | grep sidecar-injector
Verify webhook configuration:
kubectl get mutatingwebhookconfiguration istio-sidecar-injector
